---
// Lesson 1.1 — Launch: Fork, Clone & Press Play
// Day one: every student forks the starter, clones it, opens it in Unity,
// and presses Play. The world is drawn, three scenes are wired to load one
// another, and the player already breathes an idle animation — but there is
// no game logic anywhere in the project. Not one script does anything.
// Students will build every mechanic themselves.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.1 — Launch: Fork, Clone &amp; Press Play</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Today you get the world you will bring to life over the next four months.
    The sprites are drawn, the tilemaps are laid out, and your character is already
    breathing an idle animation. But press Play and try to move: <strong>nothing happens</strong>.
    That is not a bug you are hunting — it is the whole point. Every script in this
    project is an empty shell, and you are going to fill them in yourself by reading
    Unity's documentation, breaking big problems into smaller ones, and building one
    mechanic at a time.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Setup Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — Fork the starter.</strong> Log into GitHub, open the
        <code>cs-curriculum-main</code> repo your teacher links in Canvas, and click
        <strong>Fork</strong> (top right). A fork is a copy of the repo that belongs
        to <em>you</em> — your commits go to your copy, and you can never break the original.
    </p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Fork vs. clone — say it out loud:</strong> <em>fork</em> copies the
        repo on GitHub (server to server, yours forever). <em>Clone</em> downloads a
        repo to this machine (server to laptop). You do both today: fork first, then
        clone your fork.
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — Clone your fork.</strong> On your fork's page click the green
        <strong>Code</strong> button, copy the HTTPS URL, then in GitHub Desktop:
        File → Clone Repository → URL tab → paste → <strong>Clone</strong>. Or in a terminal:
    </p>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/cs-curriculum-main.git</code></pre>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — Open it in Unity.</strong> Open Unity Hub → <strong>Add</strong>
        → pick your cloned <code>cs-curriculum-main</code> folder. The first open takes
        a while: Unity imports every sprite and audio file in the project. When it
        finishes, find <code>Assets/_Scenes</code> in the Project window. There are
        three scenes, and they are already wired to load one another:
        <strong>Start</strong>, <strong>Overworld</strong>, and <strong>Platformer</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — Press Play.</strong> Open the <strong>Overworld</strong> scene
        and press the Play button at the top of the editor. Watch your character in the
        middle of the screen.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: A world with no code in it</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What is your character doing the moment Play mode starts? Does the sprite sit frozen, or is an animation playing? Describe exactly what you see.</li>
            <li>Press W, A, S, D, and the arrow keys. Does the character move across the grass at all?</li>
            <li>Look at the turrets on the map. Do they rotate, notice you, or fire anything? What does that tell you about the difference between <em>graphics</em> and <em>gameplay</em>?</li>
            <li>There is exactly one coin on this map. If the character cannot move, why can that coin not be collected yet?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tour</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>What is actually in this repo?</strong> Find each of these in the
        Project window, and read the third column carefully — it tells you what is
        really there:
    </p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Folder</th><th>What lives there</th><th>Current state</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Assets/_Scenes</code></td>
            <td>Start, Overworld, Platformer</td>
            <td>Three built scenes, already wired to load each other</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Assets/Scripts</code></td>
            <td>PlayerController, EnemyController, TurretController, Projectile, StatManager, Lever</td>
            <td><strong>Every one is an empty shell</strong> — a class with blank methods. Nothing runs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Assets/Prefabs</code></td>
            <td>Player, Coin, Spikes, MobileEnemy, Turret, Turret_Projectile, AxeItem, Switch</td>
            <td>Pictures with tags. No physics, no behaviour.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Assets/Artwork</code></td>
            <td>Top-down grass and stone tiles, cave tilesets, characters, weapons</td>
            <td>All the art is finished and ready to use</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Assets/Animations</code></td>
            <td>The coin's spin animation</td>
            <td>Already playing — the coin is the one thing that looks alive</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Assets/Scripts/Package Scripts</code></td>
            <td>CameraController, SceneSwitch, TopDown_EnemyAnimator</td>
            <td><strong>The only working code you inherit.</strong> The camera follows you and the caves load — that is it.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        <strong>Why so empty?</strong> Because reading a finished game and nodding along
        teaches you almost nothing. Instead, this year you take real game behaviours —
        collecting a coin, taking damage, aiming a turret, jumping — and break each one
        into small answerable questions:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>What Unity event fires when two shapes touch? (You will read <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code>)</li>
        <li>How is a harmful spike different from a friendly coin? (Triggers versus collisions)</li>
        <li>What maths finds the distance from a turret to its target? (You will read <code>Vector3.Distance</code>)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your character already plays an idle animation without a single line of your code. Who or what is driving that animation right now?</li>
            <li>Why is building a game system yourself from documentation more useful for learning computer science than reading a finished project and answering questions about what each line does?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Ship It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Commit — the habit that starts today.</strong> In GitHub Desktop
        (or <code>git add</code> / <code>git commit -m</code> / <code>git push</code>),
        commit with the message <code>"1.1: cloned the starter"</code> and push.
        From now on, <strong>every single class ends with a commit and a push.</strong>
        Your commit history is the proof of your progress — and in June you will read
        it back to see how far you came.
    </p>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain the difference between a fork and a clone</li>
            <li>Open the Overworld scene in Unity and enter Play mode</li>
            <li>Say what your character does on Play, and confirm it cannot move yet</li>
            <li>Name the three scenes and find <code>Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs</code> in the Project window</li>
            <li>Commit and push your work to your own GitHub repository</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the foundational tools:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — the editor tour, scripts, input, and vectors</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — every API this course uses</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="1.2.html">1.2 — The Unity Editor Tour</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.1" title="1.1 Launch: Fork, Clone &amp; Press Play — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
